1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:05,380
多年来，杨立昆一直在告诉整个 AI 行业，他们正在犯个巨大的错误。
For years, Jan Lacun has been telling the entire AI industry that it's making a huge mistake.

2
00:00:05,774 --> 00:00:14,628
就在 OpenAI、Google、Anthropic 和 Meta 花几千亿美元把
大语言模型做得越来越大时，杨立昆一直在重复同样的一点。
While OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, and Meta spent hundreds of billions of dollars
making large language models bigger and bigger, Lacun kept saying the same thing.

3
00:00:15,054 --> 00:00:17,924
更大的聊天机器人并不会带我们走向人类水平智能。
Bigger chatbots will not get us to human-level intelligence.

4
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:19,840
但大多数人都忽略了这一点。
And most people ignored it.

5
00:00:20,174 --> 00:00:25,348
不过，杨立昆刚刚发表了一篇新论文，这可能是迄今为止证明他正确的最强有力证据。
But Lacun has just published a new paper that might be the strongest evidence yet that Lacun was right.

6
00:00:25,678 --> 00:00:33,360
在论文中，一个只有 1 亿参数的模型在一项非常重要的测试中，击败了高达 720 亿参数的模型。
In it, a model with just 109 million parameters beat models up to 72 billion parameters on one very important test.

7
00:00:33,774 --> 00:00:36,000
这个模型比后者小了 600 多倍。
A model more than 600 times smaller.

8
00:00:36,334 --> 00:00:40,964
如果这个思路在规模化后依然成立，它可能会改变下一代 AI 的构建方式。
And if this idea holds up when it's scaled, it could change how the next generation of AI is built.

9
00:00:41,390 --> 00:00:44,164
但首先，这里是杨立昆多年来一直坚持的观点。
But first, here's the claim Lacun has been making for years.

10
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:49,060
我们不可能仅仅通过做大 LLMs 就达到人类水平的 AI。
We are not going to get to human-level AI by just getting up LLMs.

11
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:51,044
这根本不可能实现。
This is just not going to happen.

12
00:00:51,278 --> 00:00:51,520
好吧。
Okay.

13
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:52,740
那是你的观点。
That's your perspective.

14
00:00:53,358 --> 00:00:54,084
根本不可能。
There's no way.

15
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:55,748
没错，绝对不可能。
Okay, absolutely no way.

16
00:00:57,806 --> 00:01:05,604
而且正如你们从我一些更有冒险精神的同事那里听到的那样，这在未来两年内不会发生。
and whatever you can hear from some of my more adventurous colleagues, it's not going to happen within the next two years.

17
00:01:06,094 --> 00:01:09,124
这绝对不可能，你知道的，原谅我的粗口。
There's absolutely no way in hell to, you know, pardon my French.

18
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:16,324
嗯，就是那种，你知道的，认为我们能在数据中心拥有一个天才国家的想法，完全是胡扯。
Um the you know, the idea that we're going to have, you know, a country of genius in the data center, that's complete BS.

19
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:17,764
绝对不可能。
There's absolutely no way.

20
00:01:22,062 --> 00:01:28,452
所以在这段视频里，我将用通俗易懂的语言解释杨立昆到底发现了什么，
以及如果这个思路被规模化，意味着什么。
So in this video, I'm going to explain what Lacoon actually found in plain English and what it could mean if this idea is scaled up.

21
00:01:28,782 --> 00:01:33,200
但要理解为什么这篇论文如此重要，你首先需要知道背后是谁。
But to understand why this paper matters so much, you first need to know who's behind it.

22
00:01:33,422 --> 00:01:38,180
这就引出了第一点：杨立昆是谁，以及为什么我一直在谈论他。
Which leads me to point number one, which is who Yan Lacoon is and why I keep talking about him.

23
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:42,884
如果你关注这个频道有一段时间了，你会知道我拍过不少关于杨立昆的视频。
If you've watched this channel for a while, you'll know I've made quite a few videos about Lan Lacoon.

24
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:46,880
今年早些时候，我报道了杨立昆对整个机器人行业的抨击。
Earlier this year, I covered Lacoon calling out the entire robotics industry.

25
00:01:47,150 --> 00:01:55,588
我做过一个关于杨立昆警告大语言模型存在危险问题的视频，
还报道了他公开批评埃隆·马斯克的 X AI。
I made a video about Lacun's warning about a dangerous problem with large language models,
and I covered Lacoon's very public criticism of Elon Musk's X AI.

26
00:01:55,790 --> 00:01:59,600
但很多人可能会纳闷，为什么我一直在谈论同一个人？
But a lot of people might be wondering, why do I keep talking about this one person?

27
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:03,600
因为在 AI 领域，杨立昆不仅仅是互联网上的又一个观点。
Because when it comes to AI, Lacun isn't just another opinion on the internet.

28
00:02:03,982 --> 00:02:07,040
杨立昆是发明现代 AI 的核心人物之一。
Jan Lacun is one of the people who invented modern AI.

29
00:02:07,310 --> 00:02:13,220
早在 1980 年代末，在 Bell Labs，杨立昆帮助开创了所谓的卷积神经网络。
Back in the late 1980s, at Bell Labs, Lacun helped pioneer something called a convolutional neural network.

30
00:02:13,486 --> 00:02:16,644
这是一种特别擅长理解图像的 AI 类型。
It's a type of AI that's especially good at understanding images.

31
00:02:17,038 --> 00:02:25,924
最早的大规模应用之一是识别手写数字，到 90 年代末，基
于杨立昆工作的系统已经能识别美国大部分支票上的内容。
One of the first big uses was reading handwritten numbers, and by the late 1990s,
systems built on Lacun's work were reading a large share of the checks written in the United States.

32
00:02:26,382 --> 00:02:34,116
如今，同样的底层逻辑就支撑着手机的人脸识别、医学图像分析，以及自动驾驶汽车的摄像头。
Today, that same basic idea sits underneath things like face recognition on
your phone, medical image analysis, and the cameras in self-driving cars.

33
00:02:34,510 --> 00:02:42,160
2018 年，杨立昆与杰弗里·辛顿和约书亚·本吉奥共同获得了图灵奖，
这基本上就是计算机科学界的诺贝尔奖。
In 2018, Lacun won the Turing Award, which is basically the Nobel
Prize of Computer Science, alongside Jeffrey Hinton and Yoshua Bengio.

34
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:45,280
他们三人常被称为 AI 教父。
The three of them are often called the godfathers of AI.

35
00:02:45,582 --> 00:02:55,716
杨立昆在 2013 年创立了 Facebook 的 AI 研究实验室，并在纽约大学任教的同时，
担任 Meta 首席人工智能科学家超过十年。
Lacoon went on to found Facebook's AI Research Lab in 2013 and spent more than a decade as Meta's chief AI scientist,
while also being a professor at New York University.

36
00:02:56,174 --> 00:02:59,760
然而，在去年年底，杨立昆做了一件非常不同寻常的事情。
And then, at the end of last year, Lacun did something very unusual.

37
00:03:00,142 --> 00:03:10,116
杨立昆离开了 Meta——全球最富有的 AI 公司之一，去巴黎创办
了一家名为 AMI Labs 的新公司，全称是高级机器智能实验室。
Lacoon walked away from Meta, one of the richest AI companies in the world, to start a new company in Paris called AMI Labs,
which stands for Advanced Machine Intelligence.

38
00:03:10,734 --> 00:03:13,732
这就是杨立昆在今年早些时候在达沃斯论坛上解释这一决定的原因。
Here's how Lacoon explained that decision at Davos earlier this year.

39
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:18,640
正如他们所说，AI 行业完全陷入了 LLM 沉溺。
The AI industry is completely LLM pilled, as they say.

40
00:03:33,582 --> 00:03:35,012
你知道，就是怕落后于竞争对手。
You know, fall behind the other guys.

41
00:03:35,534 --> 00:03:36,832
所以他们都在做同样的事情。
And so they're all doing the same thing.

42
00:03:36,992 --> 00:03:39,232
这也是我离开 Meta 的一个主要原因，对吧？
And it's one big reason I left Meta, right?

43
00:03:39,312 --> 00:03:41,792
因为 Meta 也陷入了 LLM 沉溺。
Because Meta also became L impilled.

44
00:03:45,486 --> 00:03:51,392
这没问题，你知道，这可能是他们合理的战略决策，只是不是我感兴趣的方向。
And it's fine, you know, it's a strategic decision that maybe makes sense for them, just not what I'm interested in.

45
00:03:51,662 --> 00:03:53,392
他们都在挖同样的战壕。
They're all digging the same trench.

46
00:03:53,710 --> 00:03:55,300
这就是杨立昆的核心批评。
That's Lacoon's big criticism.

47
00:03:55,630 --> 00:04:01,444
硅谷的每个人都在做同样的事情，他们不敢尝试不同的路径，因为他们怕落后。
Everyone in Silicon Valley is working on the same thing, and they can't afford to try something different because they'll fall behind.

48
00:04:01,934 --> 00:04:10,180
而在 3 月份，AMI Labs 在首轮融资中筹集了 10.3 亿美元，
这是欧洲有史以来规模最大的首轮融资之一。
And in March, AMI Labs raised $1.03 billion in its first funding round, one of the biggest first rounds Europe has ever seen.

49
00:04:10,606 --> 00:04:15,236
支持者包括杰夫·贝佐斯的投资公司、Nvidia、Samsung 和 Toyota。
The backers include Jeff Bezos' investment firm, Nvidia, Samsung, and Toyota.

50
00:04:15,726 --> 00:04:18,372
所以，这并不是某位教授在场外呐喊。
So this isn't one professor shouting from the sidelines.

51
00:04:18,733 --> 00:04:24,836
而是现代 AI 的创始人之一，拿着 10 亿美元的资金，押注于整个行业已经走偏了。
It's one of the founders of modern AI, with a billion dollars, betting that the whole industry has taken a wrong turn.

52
00:04:25,262 --> 00:04:30,052
这就引出了我的第二点，也就是杨立昆认为 AI 行业正在犯的错误。
Which leads me to point number two, which is the mistake Lacoon says the AI industry is making.

53
00:04:30,510 --> 00:04:34,500
要理解杨立昆的论点，你得先了解现在的聊天机器人是如何学习的。
To understand Lacoon's argument, you need to understand how today's chatbots learn.

54
00:04:34,926 --> 00:04:44,164
像 ChatGPT、Claude 和 Gemini 这样的大语言模型，主要是通过阅读海量的文本，
并不断提高预测下一个词的准确率来学习的。
Large language models like ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini mostly learn by reading
enormous amounts of text and getting better and better at predicting the next word.

55
00:04:44,590 --> 00:04:46,752
事实证明，这极其强大。
That turns out to be incredibly powerful.

56
00:04:47,086 --> 00:04:51,300
正是通过这种方式，这些系统才能写文章、写代码，并回答几乎任何问题。
It's how these systems can write essays, code, and answer almost any question.

57
00:04:51,758 --> 00:04:54,404
但杨立昆认为，仅靠文本本身存在硬性限制。
But Lacoon thinks text alone has a hard limit.

58
00:04:54,766 --> 00:04:57,792
杨立昆有一个非常简单的计算来解释原因。
And Lacoon has a really simple calculation that explains why.

59
00:05:15,632 --> 00:05:19,472
那是大约 10 的 14 次方字节，也就是 1 后面跟着 14 个零。
That's about 10 to the 14 bytes, a one with 14 zeros behind it.

60
00:05:19,632 --> 00:05:22,752
这就是互联网上所有公开可用的文本总量。
This is the totality of all the text available publicly on the internet.

61
00:05:23,072 --> 00:05:26,592
我们要花好几十万年才能读完这些资料。
It would take any of us several hundred thousand years to read through that material.

62
00:05:26,798 --> 00:05:28,548
好，所以这是海量的信息。
Okay, so it's an enormous amount of information.

63
00:05:28,752 --> 00:05:36,032
但如果你把这和孩子出生前四年通过视觉系统接收到的信息量相比，你会发现差不多一样多。
But then you compare this with the amount of information that gets to our brain through
the visual system in the first four years of life, and it's about the same amount.

64
00:05:36,192 --> 00:05:40,432
在四年里，一个小孩一共清醒了大约 16,000 小时。
In four years, a young child has been awake a total of about 16,000 hours.

65
00:05:40,592 --> 00:05:45,700
通过视神经进入大脑的信息量大约是每秒 2MB。
The amount of information getting to the brain through the optic nerve is about two megabytes per second.

66
00:05:45,902 --> 00:05:48,196
算一下，大约就是 10 的 14 次方字节。
Do the calculation, and that's about 10 to the 14 bytes.

67
00:05:48,462 --> 00:05:49,028
基本差不多。
It's about the same.

68
00:05:49,232 --> 00:05:54,592
在四年里，一个小孩看到的信息或数据量和最大的 LLMs 一样多。
In four years, a young child has seen as much information or data as the biggest LLMs.

69
00:05:54,832 --> 00:05:58,592
这说明了什么？说明我们不可能仅仅通过训练文本就能达到人类水平的 AI。
what that tells you is that we're never going to get to human-level AI by just training on text.

70
00:05:58,734 --> 00:06:02,912
我们必须让系统去理解现实世界，而理解现实世界是非常困难的。
We're going to have to get systems to understand the real world, and that understanding the real world is really hard.

71
00:06:03,072 --> 00:06:08,612
我认为这是整个 AI 领域最重要的思想之一，而且它出奇地容易理解。
I think that's one of the most important ideas in all of AI, and it's surprisingly easy to understand.

72
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:16,592
一个四岁的孩子通过眼睛吸收的信息量，和最大的
AI 模型从互联网上所有文本中吸收的信息量差不多。
A four year old child has taken in about as much information through their
eyes as the biggest AI models have taken in from all the text on the internet.

73
00:06:17,166 --> 00:06:21,636
但孩子学到了一些那些模型没学到的东西：世界究竟是如何运作的。
But the child has learned something those models haven't: how the world actually works.

74
00:06:22,190 --> 00:06:29,700
比如东西掉下去会摔落，球滚到沙发后面依然存在，如果你把玻璃杯从桌上推下去，它可能会碎。
That things fall when you drop them, that a ball rolling behind a sofa
still exists, that if you push a glass off a table, it will probably break.

75
00:06:30,318 --> 00:06:33,412
而且孩子在没读过一个字的情况下就学会了这一切。
And the child learned all of that without reading a single word.

76
00:06:33,902 --> 00:06:37,392
杨立昆指出，这就是为什么现在的 AI 如此奇怪的原因。
And Lacun points out that this is why AI is so strange right now.

77
00:06:37,872 --> 00:06:47,428
它解释了为什么我们拥有能通过律师资格考试、能解方程、
能像大学生一样计算积分或解决数学问题的 LLMs。
It explains why we have LLMs that can pass the bar exam or solve equations
or compute integrals like college students and solve math problems.

78
00:06:48,046 --> 00:06:52,592
但我们仍然没有能帮你做家务的家用机器人。
But we still don't have a domestic robot that can, you know, do the chores in the house.

79
00:06:52,672 --> 00:06:54,912
我们甚至还没有 L5 级别的自动驾驶汽车。
We don't we don't even have level 5 self-driving cars.

80
00:06:54,992 --> 00:06:56,432
我是说，我们有，但我们是在作弊。
I mean we have them, but we cheat.

81
00:06:56,752 --> 00:07:00,708
AI 能通过律师资格考试，但我们仍然没有能帮你收拾屋子的机器人。
AI can pass the bar exam, but we still don't have a robot that can tidy your house.

82
00:07:01,262 --> 00:07:07,172
AI 能解决数学问题，但我们还没有能像青少年一样在 20 小时内学会驾驶的自动驾驶汽车。
AI can solve maths problems, but we don't have a self-driving car that can learn to drive in 20 hours like a teenager.

83
00:07:07,662 --> 00:07:11,312
杨立昆的解释是，语言是一种非常特殊的数据。
Lacun's explanation is that language is a very special kind of data.

84
00:07:11,758 --> 00:07:20,484
所以，重点是 LLMs 在语言本身就是推理基础的领域特别成功。
So, okay, that's the important point is that LLMs are particularly successful
at domains where the language itself is the substrate of reasoning.

85
00:07:21,774 --> 00:07:22,464
但在其他领域则不然。
not for anything else.

86
00:07:22,784 --> 00:07:29,924
因此，根据杨立昆的说法，语言模型在语言本身就是
你用来推理的东西的领域非常出色，比如数学和代码。
So, according to Lacun, language models are brilliant in areas where the
language itself is the thing you're reasoning with, like maths and code.

87
00:07:30,286 --> 00:07:32,164
但现实世界不是由文字组成的。
But the real world isn't made of words.

88
00:07:32,494 --> 00:07:36,932
它是杂乱的、连续的、不可预测的，而这正是这些系统感到吃力的地方。
It's messy, continuous, and unpredictable, and that's where these systems struggle.

89
00:07:37,326 --> 00:07:41,540
杨立昆还解释了为什么今天的模型需要如此庞大。
Lacoon also has an explanation for why today's models need to be so enormous.

90
00:07:41,998 --> 00:07:48,384
在 X 上发布关于这篇新论文的帖子时，杨立昆写道，大语言模型积累了大量的陈述性知识。
In a post on X about this new paper, Lacun wrote that large language models accumulate a lot of declarative knowledge.

91
00:07:48,654 --> 00:07:50,436
基本上就是大量的、大量的各种事实。
Basically, lots and lots of facts.

92
00:07:51,054 --> 00:07:56,464
这就是它们能回答这么多问题的原因，也是它们需要这么多参数和这么多内存的原因。
That's how they can answer so many questions, and it's also why they need so many parameters and so much memory.

93
00:07:56,846 --> 00:08:05,348
但杨立昆认为，你可以用理解来取代知识的积累，而这就
是杨立昆的方法，也就是所谓的 JEPA，想要实现的目标。
But Lacun argues that you can replace the accumulation of knowledge with
understanding, and that's what Lacun's approach, called JEPA, is supposed to do.

94
00:08:05,678 --> 00:08:10,624
这就引出了第三点，也就是用通俗易懂的语言来说，这项研究背后的核心思想。
Which leads me to point number three, which is the idea behind this research in plain English.

95
00:08:11,086 --> 00:08:13,904
杨立昆提出的替代方案叫做「世界模型」。
Lacun's alternative is something called a world model.

96
00:08:14,190 --> 00:08:20,676
世界模型是一种能够建立关于世界运作方式的内部理解的 AI，这样它就能预测接下来会发生什么。
A world model is an AI that builds an internal understanding of how the world works, so it can predict what's going to happen next.

97
00:08:21,230 --> 00:08:22,404
想想接球这件事。
Think about catching a ball.

98
00:08:22,702 --> 00:08:28,036
你不需要去计算每一根草、每一阵风，还有球缝上的每一个细节。
You don't calculate every blade of grass, every gust of wind, and every detail of the ball's stitching.

99
00:08:28,366 --> 00:08:31,076
你的大脑只是大致预测球将出现在什么位置。
Your brain just predicts roughly where the ball is going to be.

100
00:08:31,534 --> 00:08:33,284
这就是杨立昆希望 AI 去做的。
That's what Lacun wants AI to do.

101
00:08:33,678 --> 00:08:37,124
而大多数 AI 系统试图实现这一点的关键问题就在这里。
And here's the key problem with the way most AI systems try to do this.

102
00:08:37,614 --> 00:08:43,744
如果你训练 AI 去逐像素预测视频的下一帧，它就必须去猜每一个微小的细节。
If you train an AI to predict the next frame of a video, pixel by pixel, it has to guess every tiny detail.

103
00:08:44,014 --> 00:08:46,788
而其中很多细节根本就是无法预测的。
And a lot of those details are simply impossible to predict.

104
00:08:47,310 --> 00:08:50,628
杨立昆在 ETH Zurich 的一场讲座中举了一个很好的例子。
Lacun gave a great example of this in a lecture at ETH Zurich.

105
00:09:01,984 --> 00:09:11,332
你知道，我们现在是在某种教室礼堂里，而且你知道，
房间的大小是有限的，这一侧可能有窗户之类的。
You know, we are in some sort of classroom auditorium, and you know, the room has a finite size,
there might be windows on this side and things like that.

106
00:09:11,630 --> 00:09:14,660
系统绝对不可能预测出你们每个人的长相。
There's absolutely no way the system can predict what all of you look like.

107
00:09:15,854 --> 00:09:17,988
或者预测出哪些椅子是空的。
Or which you know chairs are unoccupied.

108
00:09:19,342 --> 00:09:20,144
这根本就不可能。
it's just impossible.

109
00:09:20,464 --> 00:09:21,504
你根本就没有这些信息。
You just don't have the information.

110
00:09:21,584 --> 00:09:24,784
所以当你训练一个系统去做这种预测时，你就把它搞废了。
So when you train a system to make this kind of prediction, you kill it.

111
00:09:25,134 --> 00:09:26,052
这就是核心问题。
That's the core issue.

112
00:09:26,286 --> 00:09:32,580
如果你强迫 AI 去预测所有事情，包括那些无法预测的事情，它就会浪费精力并感到困惑。
If you force an AI to predict everything, including the stuff that can't be predicted, it wastes its effort and gets confused.

113
00:09:33,038 --> 00:09:40,132
所以杨立昆的解决方案是一种不同的设计，叫做 JEPA，全称是联合嵌入预测架构。
So Lacun's solution is a different kind of design called JEPA, which stands for joint embedding predictive architecture.

114
00:09:40,430 --> 00:09:41,252
这名字挺绕口的。
That's a mouthful.

115
00:09:41,344 --> 00:09:42,916
这就是杨立昆的解释方式。
So here's how Lacoon explains it.

116
00:09:43,246 --> 00:09:44,784
所以这是我的解决方案。
So here's my solution.

117
00:09:44,944 --> 00:09:55,172
我的解决方案是一个我称之为联合嵌入，或者更准确地说，
是联合嵌入预测架构，也就是 JEPA，就在右边显示。
My solution is an architecture I call uh joint embedding, or more precisely,
joint embedding predictive architecture, JEPA, which is shown on the right.

118
00:09:55,854 --> 00:10:08,164
好，左边是生成式架构，你观察 X，也许你观察 A，也就是正在发生的动作，然后你观察结果 Y，
系统正试图以最微小的细节来重构 Y。
Okay, on the left you have generative architecture, you observe X, maybe you observe A, an action that is taking place,
and you observe the result Y, and the system is trying to reconstruct Y in its most minute details.

119
00:10:09,518 --> 00:10:18,468
而通过 JEPA，你观察 X、Y 和 A，但你同时对 X 和 Y 进行编码，
预测过程发生在那个表示空间里。
with uh JEPA, you observe X and Y and A, but you encode both X and Y, and the prediction takes place in that representation space.

120
00:10:19,664 --> 00:10:19,984
明白了吗？
Okay?

121
00:10:20,878 --> 00:10:21,572
主要的区别就在这儿。
Major difference.

122
00:10:22,254 --> 00:10:35,844
系统的做法本质上是通过构建 Y 的表示，从输入中剔除掉那些根本无法预测的关于 Y 的信息。
What the system can do is essentially eliminate from the input by constructing a representation of Y,
it can eliminate all the information about Y that is simply not predictable.

123
00:10:37,358 --> 00:10:37,668
对吧？
Right?

124
00:10:38,734 --> 00:10:44,384
这让预测变得更抽象，细节更少，但在某种程度上更准确。
And that makes the prediction more abstract with fewer details, but more accurate in a way.

125
00:10:44,624 --> 00:10:54,916
简单来说，JEPA 不是去预测每一个词或每一个像素这样的原始细节，
而是先将它看到的东西转化成一种总结，也就是对现状的理解。
So, in simple terms, instead of predicting the raw details, like every word or every pixel,
JEPA first turns what it sees into a kind of summary, an idea of what's going on.

126
00:10:55,310 --> 00:10:58,020
然后它就在这个“理解空间”里进行预测。
Then it makes its predictions in that idea space.

127
00:10:58,478 --> 00:11:03,492
它可以抛弃那些不重要或无法预测的细节，专注于真正重要的内容。
It can throw away the details that don't matter or can't be predicted and focus on what does matter.

128
00:11:03,822 --> 00:11:04,704
打个比方。
Here's an analogy.

129
00:11:05,006 --> 00:11:07,812
想象两个人在描述同一场足球比赛。
Imagine two people describing the same football match.

130
00:11:08,078 --> 00:11:12,836
一个人描述每一根草的形状、每个座位的颜色，还有每个球迷穿的衣服。
One describes every blade of grass, the colour of every seat, and what every fan is wearing.

131
00:11:13,166 --> 00:11:17,604
另一个人只说，前锋正朝着球门跑，防守队员正准备铲球。
The other just says, the striker is running towards the goal and the defender is about to tackle.

132
00:11:17,934 --> 00:11:22,756
第二种描述细节少得多，但对于预测接下来会发生什么来说，它有用得多。
The second description is far less detailed, but it's much more useful for predicting what happens next.

133
00:11:23,246 --> 00:11:26,372
JEPA 就是在尝试学习这种第二种理解方式。
JEPA is trying to learn that second kind of understanding.

134
00:11:26,734 --> 00:11:31,104
这种系统有一个缺陷，值得提一下，因为论文里也提到了这一点。
There is one catch with this kind of system, and it's worth mentioning because it comes up in the paper.

135
00:11:31,374 --> 00:11:33,636
如果你不小心，AI 可能会“作弊”。
If you're not careful, the AI can cheat.

136
00:11:34,094 --> 00:11:39,396
它可能会学会给所有事物都给出一个相同的总结，因为这样它的预测在技术上永远是正确的。
It can learn to give the same summary for everything because then its predictions are always technically correct.

137
00:11:39,822 --> 00:11:40,868
这被称为“崩溃”。
That's called collapse.

138
00:11:41,390 --> 00:11:51,744
LeCun 的团队使用了 Sigreg 和 VC Reg 等特殊的训练规则，
基本上是阻止 AI 走这种偷懒的捷径，强迫它去学习真正有意义的东西。
Lacoon's team uses special training rules with names like Sigreg and VC Reg that basically stop the AI from taking that lazy shortcut
and force it to actually learn something meaningful.

139
00:11:52,078 --> 00:11:56,356
你不需要记住这些名字，只要知道这就是让 JEPA 奏效的窍门就行。
You don't need to remember the names, just know that this is the trick that makes JEPA work.

140
00:11:56,782 --> 00:12:00,548
这就引出了第四点，也就是这篇新论文的实际发现。
Which leads me to point number four, which is what this new paper actually found.

141
00:12:00,942 --> 00:12:13,188
这篇论文叫《通过 JEPA 证明并改进 Platonic Representation Hypothesis》，
作者是布朗大学教授兰德尔·巴莱斯特里奥，他曾与 LeCun 以及杨立昆在 Meta 共事。
The paper is called Proving and Improving the Platonic Representation Hypothesis with JEPAs, and it's by Randall Balestriero,
a professor at Brown University who used to work with Lacun at Meta and Jan Lacun himself.

142
00:12:13,424 --> 00:12:15,744
它是在 10 月 29 日发布的。
It was posted on the 29th of October.

143
00:12:15,918 --> 00:12:19,972
要理解它，你需要知道什么是 Platonic Representation Hypothesis。
To understand it, you need to know what the Platonic Representation Hypothesis is.

144
00:12:20,718 --> 00:12:24,900
早在 2024 年，MIT 的研究人员就注意到了一些有趣的现象。
Back in 2024, researchers at MIT noticed something fascinating.

145
00:12:25,326 --> 00:12:36,388
当他们观察不同的 AI 模型——包括在图像上训练的模型和在文本上训练的模型时，
随着模型变得越来越强大，这些模型内部组织信息的方式开始变得越来越相似。
When they looked inside different AI models, models trained on images and models trained on text,
the way those models organized information internally started to look more and more alike as the models got more powerful.

146
00:12:36,814 --> 00:12:44,996
就好像它们都在慢慢发现同一个世界的底层地图，尽
管一个模型只见过图片，而另一个模型只读过文字。
It's as if they were all slowly discovering the same underlying map of the world,
even though one had only ever seen pictures and the other had only ever read words.

147
00:12:45,614 --> 00:12:54,212
他们以希腊哲学家柏拉图的名字来命名这个假设，柏拉图认为我们看到的东西就像洞穴墙上的影
子，而影子的背后是唯一的真实世界。
They named it after Plato, the Greek philosopher, who had an idea that the things
we see are like shadows on a cave wall, and that behind them is one true reality.

148
00:12:54,702 --> 00:13:00,420
这个假设是：不同的 AI 看到的是不同的影子，但它们都在学习影子背后的同一个现实。
The hypothesis is that different AIs are seeing different shadows, but all learning the same reality behind them.

149
00:13:00,910 --> 00:13:05,252
但到目前为止，还没有人真正证明了为什么会发生这种情况，或者如何有目的地让它发生。
But nobody had actually proven why this happens or how to make it happen on purpose.

150
00:13:05,806 --> 00:13:08,004
而这就是 LeCun 和巴莱斯特里奥所做的研究。
And that's what Lacun and Balestriero did.

151
00:13:08,558 --> 00:13:22,084
他们从数学上证明了，在某些条件下，用完全不同类型数据（比如文本和图像）训练的 JEPA 模
型，最终会学习到相同的世界地图，只是旋转了角度，就像同一张地图被转了个方向一样。
They showed mathematically that, under certain conditions, JEPA models trained on completely different kinds of data,
like text and images, will end up learning the same map of the world, just rotated, like the same map turned at a different angle.

152
00:13:22,638 --> 00:13:24,996
关键条件是他们所说的「良好视角」。
The key condition is what they call good views.

153
00:13:25,646 --> 00:13:30,340
所谓良好视角，就是 AI 通过比较对同一时刻的两种不同观察来学习。
A good view is when the AI learns by comparing two different observations of the same moment.

154
00:13:30,670 --> 00:13:38,148
例如，一张狗接飞盘的照片和描述同一场景的句子，或者同一房间的两张不同照片。
For example, a photo of a dog catching a frisbee and a sentence describing that same scene, or two different photos of the same room.

155
00:13:38,670 --> 00:13:45,956
因为两者都是关于同一个现实，AI 就被推动去学习它们的共同点，也就是世界上实际发生的事件。
Because both are about the same piece of reality, the AI is pushed to learn what they have in common,
which is the actual thing happening in the world.

156
00:13:46,574 --> 00:13:51,876
而糟糕视角则是指 AI 比较的两件事并不共享相同的底层现实。
A bad view is when the two things the AI compares don't really share the same underlying reality.

157
00:13:52,302 --> 00:13:54,052
接下来是真正有趣的部分。
And here's the really interesting part.

158
00:13:54,414 --> 00:14:02,468
论文认为，预测句子中的下一个词——也就是 Chat GPT 和其
他语言模型学习的正是这种方式——实际上是一个糟糕视角。
The paper argues that predicting the next word in a sentence, which is
exactly how Chat GPT and other language models learn, is actually a bad view.

159
00:14:02,990 --> 00:14:07,392
句子中的接下来的几个词往往不会告诉你太多关于世界实际状态的信息。
The next few words in a sentence often don't tell you that much about the actual state of the world.

160
00:14:07,630 --> 00:14:15,268
论文还指出，这就是语言模型与图像模型看待世界的方式
契合度意外低的一个原因，即使它们规模巨大也是如此。
And the paper suggests that's one reason language models line up with how
image models see the world surprisingly weakly, even when they're enormous.

161
00:14:15,918 --> 00:14:17,028
然后他们进行了测试。
Then they tested it.

162
00:14:17,358 --> 00:14:28,672
在衡量文本模型的理解与图像模型看待世界的方式的契合度上，一个仅有 1.09 亿参数、
通过良好视角训练的 JEPA 模型得分是 0.184。
On a measure of how closely a text model's understanding matched the way image models see the world,
a JEPA with just 109 million parameters, trained with Goodviews, scored 0.184.

163
00:14:29,102 --> 00:14:35,472
来自 Alibaba 的 720 亿参数模型 Quen 2.5 得分是 0.154。
Quen 2.5, a 72 billion parameter model from Alibaba, scored 0.154.

164
00:14:35,918 --> 00:14:39,712
Meta 的 Llama 370B 得分是 0.151。
Meta's Llama 370B scored 0.151.

165
00:14:40,206 --> 00:14:43,620
所以一个规模小了 600 多倍的模型反而胜出了。
So a model more than 600 times smaller came out ahead.

166
00:14:44,078 --> 00:14:45,860
而且它的效率也高得多。
It was also much more efficient.

167
00:14:46,286 --> 00:14:51,716
良好视角方法只需要大约 2160 万个训练样本就能达到同样的对齐水平。
The Goodviews approach reached the same level of alignment with about 21.6 million training examples.

168
00:14:52,142 --> 00:14:54,788
而下一词预测则需要大约 9 亿个。
Next word prediction needed around 900 million.

169
00:14:55,118 --> 00:15:01,092
这大约减少了 42 倍的样本，或者说减少了约 14 倍的文本，具体取决于你怎么计算。
That's roughly 42 times fewer examples, or about 14 times less text, depending on how you count it.

170
00:15:01,518 --> 00:15:03,748
还有一个结果我觉得非常酷。
And there was one more result I found really cool.

171
00:15:04,110 --> 00:15:11,684
因为文本模型和图像模型最终得到了相同的地图，只是旋转了角度，
研究人员可以用一个简单的旋转操作将它们连接起来。
Because the text model and an image model ended up with the same map, just rotated,
the researchers could connect them with a single simple rotation.

172
00:15:12,078 --> 00:15:17,988
这就足以让这个组合变成一个能够描述图像的系统，而且不需要复杂的额外训练。
And that was enough to turn the combination into a system that could describe images, with no complicated extra training.

173
00:15:18,414 --> 00:15:21,188
LeCun 对这一意义的总结是核心关键点。
Lacoon's own summary of what this means is the key line.

174
00:15:21,582 --> 00:15:25,540
语言模型在积累知识，这就是为什么它们需要那么多内存。
Language models accumulate knowledge, which is why they need so much memory.

175
00:15:25,934 --> 00:15:32,900
JEPA 世界模型用理解取代了这种积累，这就是为什么它们可以用更少的内存和训练数据来工作。
JEPA world models replace that accumulation with understanding, which is why they can work with much less memory and training data.

176
00:15:33,326 --> 00:15:36,580
其中一位研究员阿琼·贾因用了一种我很喜欢的方式来表达。
One researcher, Arjun Jain, put it in a way I really liked.

177
00:15:37,038 --> 00:15:42,628
阿琼说，在他们读博期间，他们被训练去认为 LeCun 是错的，而且认为神经网络就是炼金术。
Arjun said that during their PhD, they were trained to think Lacoon was wrong and that neural networks were alchemy.

178
00:15:43,118 --> 00:15:46,180
然后阿琼去了纽约大学，LeCun 改变了他们的想法。
Then Arjun went to NYU and Lacoon changed their mind.

179
00:15:46,574 --> 00:15:53,316
现在，正如阿琼所说，LeCun 做了他们学校一直要求他做的一件事：他带来了证据。
And now, as Arjun put it, Lacoon did the one thing their old school always asked for Lacoon brought a proof.

180
00:15:53,934 --> 00:15:57,508
这就引出了我的第五点，也就是这篇论文没有证明的内容。
Which leads me to point number five, which is what this paper doesn't prove.

181
00:15:57,934 --> 00:16:01,540
我想把这一点说清楚，因为有些标题有点夸大其词了。
I want to be really clear about this because the headlines got a bit carried away.

182
00:16:01,934 --> 00:16:07,684
这个拥有 1.09 亿参数的模型并不是在所有方面都击败了 720 亿参数的模型。
The 109 million parameter model did not beat a 72 billion parameter model at everything.

183
00:16:08,046 --> 00:16:14,980
它只是在某一个特定指标上胜出，即它的内部理解与图像模型观察世界的方式有多契合。
It beat it on one specific measure, which is how closely its internal understanding lines up with the way image models see the world.

184
00:16:15,374 --> 00:16:20,932
这并不意味着这个小模型在数学、编程、写论文或回答常识问题上更出色。
It doesn't mean this little model is better at maths, coding, writing essays, or answering general knowledge questions.

185
00:16:21,422 --> 00:16:23,492
事实上，它甚至没在这些方面进行过测试。
In fact, it wasn't even tested on those things.

186
00:16:23,918 --> 00:16:24,944
它不是聊天机器人。
It's not a chatbot.

187
00:16:25,102 --> 00:16:27,780
Continuum Labs 的账号就对此提出了一个很好的观点。
The account Continuum Labs made a great point about this.

188
00:16:28,206 --> 00:16:36,624
1.09 亿参数模型在单项基准测试中击败 720 亿参数模型，
这个结论比说「规模时代结束了」要更具体。
A 109 million parameter model beating a 72 billion parameter one-on-one benchmark is a narrower claim than saying scale is over.

189
00:16:37,006 --> 00:16:44,868
作者舒鲁蒂也表示认同，她转发论文的帖子走红了，她说
这是世界建模方面的有力数据，而不是规模时代的终结。
And the creator Shruti, whose post about the paper went viral, agreed, saying it's
a strong data point for the world modelling side rather than the end of scale.

190
00:16:45,230 --> 00:16:46,532
这两点可以同时成立。
Both things can be true at once.

191
00:16:46,990 --> 00:16:51,684
大模型可能在广度上更强，而更好的架构可能在理解世界方面更出色。
Big models can be better at breadth, and better architectures can be better at understanding the world.

192
00:16:52,174 --> 00:16:53,700
还有其他合理的质疑。
There are other fair questions too.

193
00:16:54,030 --> 00:16:56,516
这些结果还没有经过独立的复现。
The results haven't been independently reproduced yet.

194
00:16:56,846 --> 00:17:03,204
这些数学推导仅在特定条件下成立，有些人甚至在问，这是否真的削弱了杨立昆自己的论点。
The maths only holds under specific conditions, and some people asked whether this actually undermines Lacun's own argument.

195
00:17:03,630 --> 00:17:10,242
X 上有人问，如果语言模型内部也学习了这类世界表征，那为什么要把它们排除在外？
One person on X asked, if language models also learn these kinds of world representations internally, why are they excluded?

196
00:17:10,670 --> 00:17:17,924
这是个好问题，诚实的回答是，根据这篇论文，语言模
型确实学到了一些关于世界的信息，只是效率低得多。
That's a fair question, and the honest answer is that language models do learn
something about the world, just much less efficiently, according to this paper.

197
00:17:18,286 --> 00:17:20,548
当然，杨立昆也有很多批评者。
And of course, Lacun has plenty of critics.

198
00:17:21,069 --> 00:17:28,064
有人回复说 JEPA 模型仍然没有实际应用场景，而且杨立昆似乎还停留在五年前的研究中。
One person replied that JEPA models still don't have a practical use case, and that Lacun seems stuck in research from five years ago.

199
00:17:28,526 --> 00:17:35,748
杨立昆直接回应道，人类大部分时候并不是用语言思
考，动物肯定不是用语言思考，但它们确实在思考。
Lacoon responded directly, writing that humans mostly don't think in words,
and that animals certainly don't think in words, but they do think.

200
00:17:36,270 --> 00:17:42,180
杨立昆还指出了近期的演讲和论文，给那些认为过去五年没任何进展的人看看。
Lacun also pointed the critic to recent talks and papers, for anyone who thinks nothing has happened in the last five years.

201
00:17:42,766 --> 00:17:46,404
那么，杨立昆是不是刚刚证明了整个 AI 行业都是错的？
So, did Jan Lakun just prove the entire AI industry wrong?

202
00:17:46,862 --> 00:17:54,884
并非完全如此，但杨立昆证明了为什么行业的主流
方法可能如此低效，并展示了一个可行的替代方案。
Not completely, but Lacun has proven something important about why the
industry's main approach might be so inefficient and shown a working alternative.

203
00:17:55,214 --> 00:17:56,304
这意义重大。
And that's a big deal.

204
00:17:56,686 --> 00:18:00,868
这就引出了第六点：如果把这个方案扩大规模会发生什么？
Which leads me to point number six, which is what happens if this is scaled up?

205
00:18:01,358 --> 00:18:03,140
这才是最令人兴奋的地方。
This is where it gets really exciting.

206
00:18:03,534 --> 00:18:06,692
目前，AI 行业的主流策略是规模。
Right now, the AI industry's main strategy is scale.

207
00:18:07,024 --> 00:18:10,704
更多的数据、更多的芯片、更多的数据中心、更多的资金。
More data, more chips, more data centers, more money.

208
00:18:11,022 --> 00:18:15,908
如果让 AI 更聪明的唯一方法就是把它做大，那么谁最有钱谁就赢了。
And if the only way to make AI smarter is to make it bigger, then whoever has the most money wins.

209
00:18:16,430 --> 00:18:19,684
但这篇论文表明，可能存在一条效率更高的路径。
But this paper suggests there might be a much more efficient path.

210
00:18:20,046 --> 00:18:28,132
如果你能用少 42 倍的数据，并用小几百倍的模型获得同样的理解力，
那么 AI 的整个经济模型就变了。
If you can get the same understanding from 42 times less data and with
a model hundreds of times smaller, the whole economics of AI changes.

211
00:18:28,558 --> 00:18:29,700
想想这意味着什么。
Think about what that could mean.

212
00:18:30,030 --> 00:18:36,260
理解世界的 AI 可以运行在手机、眼镜或机器人上，而不是在巨大的数据中心里。
AI that understands the world could run on a phone, a pair of glasses, or a robot instead of in a giant data center.

213
00:18:36,526 --> 00:18:44,304
训练成本可能会大幅下降，规模较小的实验室、大学，
甚至是没有万亿资金支持的国家，也能构建出强大的 AI。
Training costs could drop massively, and smaller labs, universities, and
even countries without trillion dollar budgets could build serious AI.

214
00:18:44,622 --> 00:18:47,268
而且杨立昆的实验室已经展示了一些初步迹象。
And Lacun's lab has already shown some early hints of this.

215
00:18:47,630 --> 00:18:57,540
今年 3 月，AMI Labs 发布了一个叫 Le World Model 的模型，
这是一个只有 1500 万参数的世界模型，仅用单张显卡在几小时内就完成了图像训练。
In March, AMI Labs released something called Le World Model, a world model with just 15 million parameters that was trained from
images in a matter of hours on a single graphics card.

216
00:18:57,774 --> 00:19:02,532
在某些控制任务中，它的规划速度甚至能比大得多的世界模型快 48 倍。
In some control tasks, it could plan up to 48 times faster than much bigger world models.

217
00:19:02,862 --> 00:19:13,184
到了 10 月，AMI Labs 又增加了一个叫 HJEPA 的层级版本，它能在不同的时间尺度上进行预测，
有点就像你从宏观上规划全天，又从微观上规划下一步一样。
And in October, AMI Labs added a hierarchical version called HJEPA that predicts at different timescales,
a bit like how you plan your day at a high level and your next step at a low level.

218
00:19:13,390 --> 00:19:18,544
在一次导航测试中，成功率从简单版本的 18% 提升到了 73%。
On one navigation test, success went from 18% for the simpler version to 73%.

219
00:19:19,150 --> 00:19:21,156
但最大的影响在于机器人。
But the biggest implication is robots.

220
00:19:21,582 --> 00:19:26,212
杨立昆反复说过，我们现在的机器人智能程度甚至还没到猫的水平。
Lacun has said again and again that we don't have robots anywhere near as smart as a cat.

221
00:19:28,144 --> 00:19:37,600
我的意思是，家猫对物理世界的理解简直令人惊叹，而它们大脑里只有 8 亿个神经元，好吗。
I mean, house cats have an understanding of the physical world that is completely amazing,
and they only have two billion new uh 800 million neurons, okay, in their brain.

222
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:40,240
而且它们显然对物理世界有非常深刻的理解。
And they certainly have a very good understanding of the physical world.

223
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:43,920
它们能对复杂的动作进行复杂的规划。
They can do complex planning of complex actions.

224
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:46,480
而我们离匹配这种能力还差得很远。
And we are nowhere near matching this.

225
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:52,132
家猫可以跳上柜子，完美判断距离，并规划出穿过房间的复杂路线。
House cat can jump onto a counter, judge the distance perfectly, and plan a complicated route through a room.

226
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:56,800
它用比人类少得多的神经元完成了这一切，而且它从来没读过任何文字。
It does all of this with far fewer neurons than a human and without ever reading a word.

227
00:19:57,102 --> 00:20:08,516
如果像 JEPA 这样的世界模型能通过观察和预测世界来学习，就像动物那样学习，
那它可能就是机器人、自动驾驶汽车以及能在物理世界提供实际帮助的 AI 助手的缺失拼图。
If world models like JEPA can learn the way animals learn by watching and predicting the world,
that could be the missing piece for robots, self-driving cars, and AI assistants that can actually help in the physical world.

228
00:20:08,782 --> 00:20:10,532
而且你已经可以看到这个方向的走向了。
And you can already see where this is going.

229
00:20:10,830 --> 00:20:22,852
就在本月，来自 Columbia、UC Berkeley、University of Michigan 和 NVIDIA 的研究人员展示
了一个 3D 世界模拟器，这是一个能预测布料和软物体在机器人触摸时如何移动的世界模型。
Just this month, researchers from Columbia, UC Berkeley, the University of Michigan, and NVIDIA showed a 3D world simulator,
a world model that predicts how things like cloth and soft objects move when a robot touches them.

230
00:20:23,086 --> 00:20:28,292
仅在那个模拟世界中学习的机器人，随后就能在现实世界中完成任务。
Robots that learned only inside that simulated world were then able to do the task in the real world.

231
00:20:28,526 --> 00:20:33,796
而且它在单张显卡上能跑出每秒 50 多帧，速度足够快，可以直接投入使用。
And it runs at more than 50 frames a second on a single graphics card, so it's fast enough to actually use.

232
00:20:34,158 --> 00:20:38,560
这就引出了我的第七点，即这是否可能是通往真正 AGI 的路径。
Which leads me to point number seven, which is whether this could be the path to real AGI.

233
00:20:38,734 --> 00:20:48,324
AGI，也就是通用人工智能，是指一种能够完成人类几乎任何思维任务的 AI，
而杨立昆对缺失的内容有非常明确的看法。
AGI, or artificial general intelligence, is the idea of an AI that can do pretty much any thinking task a human can,
and Lacun has a very specific view of what's missing.

234
00:20:48,878 --> 00:20:53,252
在 NVIDIA 的会议上，杨立昆列出了他认为真正重要的四件事。
At NVIDIA's conference, Lacun laid out the four things that Lacun thinks really matter.

235
00:20:53,860 --> 00:20:59,440
我觉得这四件事中包含了一些更有趣的问题。
I think there are more interesting questions in four things.

236
00:20:59,790 --> 00:21:01,920
你如何让机器理解物理世界？
How do you get machines to understand the physical world?

237
00:21:02,254 --> 00:21:05,092
Jensen 今天早上在主题演讲中谈到了这一点。
And Jensen talked about this this morning in this keynote.

238
00:21:05,550 --> 00:21:10,020
你如何让它们拥有持久记忆，这是不是不是太多人讨论的？
How do you get them to have persistent memory, which not too many people talk about?

239
00:21:10,446 --> 00:21:13,840
最后两点是，你如何让它们具备推理和规划能力？
And then the last two are how do you get them to reason and plan?

240
00:21:13,920 --> 00:21:24,480
当然，目前有一些努力在尝试让 LLMs 进行推理，但在我看来，
这是对推理的一种非常简单化的看法。
And there is some effort, of course, to get LLMs to reason, but in
my opinion, it's a very kind of simplistic way of viewing reasoning.

241
00:21:24,640 --> 00:21:28,836
我觉得可能还有更好的做法。
I think there are probably kind of more you know better way of doing this.

242
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:37,668
所以我对那些可能在五年后让技术社区很多人感到兴奋的事情感到兴奋。
so I'm excited about things that a lot of people in this community,
in the tech community, might get excited about five years from now.

243
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:44,400
但目前看起来并不那么令人兴奋，因为它只是些晦涩的学术论文。
but right now it doesn't look so exciting because it's some obscure academic paper.

244
00:21:44,750 --> 00:21:49,092
理解物理世界、持久记忆、推理和规划。
Understanding the physical world, persistent memory, reasoning, and planning.

245
00:21:49,486 --> 00:21:54,500
注意杨立昆最后提到的那些今天看起来像晦涩学术论文的想法。
And notice what Lacun said at the end about the ideas that look like an obscure academic paper today.

246
00:21:54,990 --> 00:21:58,052
嗯，这正是其中一篇晦涩的学术论文。
Well, this is exactly one of those obscure academic papers.

247
00:21:58,414 --> 00:22:01,476
大多数在 X 上刷到它的人可能意识不到它意味着什么。
Most people scrolling past it on X won't realize what it might mean.

248
00:22:02,030 --> 00:22:04,100
那么，这会是通往 AGI 的路径吗？
So, could this be the path to AGI?

249
00:22:04,590 --> 00:22:05,508
这是我真实的看法。
Here's my honest view.

250
00:22:05,934 --> 00:22:07,108
单凭它本身，不行。
On its own, no.

251
00:22:07,534 --> 00:22:09,508
这篇论文只是拼图中的一块。
This paper is one piece of the puzzle.

252
00:22:09,774 --> 00:22:12,800
它展示了如何让 AI 更高效地理解世界。
It shows how to make an AI understand the world more efficiently.

253
00:22:13,070 --> 00:22:16,036
它本身并不能解决记忆、推理和规划问题。
It doesn't solve memory, reasoning, and planning by itself.

254
00:22:16,590 --> 00:22:25,860
但如果你把一个真正理解世界的模型，与记忆、规划能力结合起来，
并且能足够高效地实现规模化，那么你就会得到一个比我
But if you combine a model that truly understands the world, with memory, with the ability to plan,
and you can do it efficiently enough to scale, then you'd have something that looks a lot more like the kind of intelligence we see in

255
00:22:25,860 --> 00:22:27,740
们今天拥有的任何东西都更接近人类和动物智能的东西。
But if you combine a model that truly understands the world, with memory, with the ability to plan,
and you can do it efficiently enough to scale, then you'd have something that looks a lot more like the kind of intelligence we see in

256
00:22:27,740 --> 00:22:30,080
们今天拥有的任何东西都更接近人类和动物智能的东西。
humans and animals than anything we have today.

257
00:22:30,478 --> 00:22:33,444
杨立昆已经明确表示，这就是 AMI Labs 的目标。
And Lacun has been clear that this is the goal of AMI Labs.

258
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:39,600
能够理解世界、拥有持久记忆、能够推理和规划，并且安全可控的系统。
Systems that understand the world, have persistent memory, can reason and plan, and are safe and controllable.

259
00:22:40,078 --> 00:22:44,452
杨立昆还说，AI 领域的人已经犯了大约 70 年的同样错误。
Lacun also says people in AI have made the same mistake for about 70 years.

260
00:22:45,006 --> 00:22:51,812
70 年来，人们一直假设如果机器能做我们觉得困难
的事情，比如下棋或微积分，那它就一定是智能的。
For 70 years, people have assumed that if a machine can do something we find hard, like chess or calculus, it must be intelligent.

261
00:22:52,206 --> 00:22:59,332
但我们觉得容易的事情，比如穿过房间或拿起杯子，结果对机器来说却是最难的事情。
But the things we find easy, like walking across a room or picking up a cup, turn out to be the hardest things of all for machines.

262
00:22:59,758 --> 00:23:02,916
而这正是杨立昆正试图构建的那种智能。
And that's exactly the kind of intelligence Lacoon is trying to build.

263
00:23:03,438 --> 00:23:07,972
这就引出了我的第八点，也就是这项研究的另一个重大影响。
Which leads me to point number eight, which is the other big implications of this research.

264
00:23:08,334 --> 00:23:10,116
首先是数据墙。
The first is the data wall.

265
00:23:10,574 --> 00:23:14,560
AI 公司已经用掉了互联网上大部分高质量的文本。
AI companies have already used up most of the good quality text on the internet.

266
00:23:15,022 --> 00:23:17,444
如果你的 AI 是从文本中学习的，那这就是一个真实的问题。
That's a real problem if your AI learns from text.

267
00:23:18,030 --> 00:23:21,700
但视频、图像和传感器数据几乎是无限的。
But video, images, and sensor data are almost unlimited.

268
00:23:22,080 --> 00:23:26,148
每一个摄像头、每一个机器人和每一辆汽车都在不断产生这些数据。
Every camera, every robot, and every car is producing it constantly.

269
00:23:26,574 --> 00:23:32,228
如果 JEPA 式的模型能从这类数据中高效学习，那么数据墙就不再是障碍了。
If JEPA style models can learn efficiently from that kind of data, the data wall stops being a wall.

270
00:23:32,750 --> 00:23:34,788
第二个是关于 AI 泡沫的争论。
The second is the AI bubble debate.

271
00:23:35,278 --> 00:23:38,628
杨立昆说过存在泡沫，但不是大多数人所认为的那种。
Lacun has said there's a bubble, but not the kind most people think.

272
00:23:39,150 --> 00:23:46,596
在其中一个环节，杨立昆说在某些方面，我们根本不在泡沫中，
因为利用语言模型仍有很多有用的东西可以构建。
In one panel, Lacun said that in some ways, we're not in a bubble at all,
because there are so many useful things still to build with language models.

273
00:23:47,040 --> 00:23:53,348
但从另一方面来说，我们确实处于泡沫中，这种观
点是认为目前的路径最终能推到人类水平的智能。
But in another way, we are, and it's the idea that the current approach will be pushed all the way to human level intelligence.

274
00:23:53,998 --> 00:24:00,420
杨立昆的观点是，数据中心的投资并没有浪费，因为每天都有数十亿人会使用AI。
Lacoon's point is that the investment in data centers isn't wasted because billions of people will use AI every day.

275
00:24:00,782 --> 00:24:06,436
但杨立昆并不相信，仅仅通过推动当前的路径就能获得人类水平的智能。
But the idea that just pushing the current approach will give us human level intelligence, Lacun doesn't believe that.

276
00:24:06,830 --> 00:24:12,132
如果高效的世界模型能够大规模应用，那么今天一些巨额投注背后的某些假设可能会改变。
And if efficient world models work at scale, some of the assumptions behind today's huge bets could change.

277
00:24:12,782 --> 00:24:14,980
第三点是即插即用AI。
The third is plug and play AI.

278
00:24:15,438 --> 00:24:24,644
论文显示，分别训练的两个模型，一个用于文本，一个用于图像，可以通过简单的旋转连接起来，
因为它们学习到了相同的世界地图。
The paper showed that separately trained models, one for text and one for images, could be connected with a single, simple rotation,
because they'd learn the same map of the world.

279
00:24:25,102 --> 00:24:35,076
如果这个想法可以扩展，你可以想象出由独立部分构建、分别训练、
然后直接拼合在一起的AI系统，而不是一个在所有内容上同时训练的巨型模型。
If that idea scales, you could imagine AI systems built from separate parts, trained separately, that just fit together,
rather than one giant model trained on everything at once.

280
00:24:35,726 --> 00:24:37,316
第四点是竞争。
And the fourth is competition.

281
00:24:37,806 --> 00:24:40,452
杨立昆不是唯一在押注世界模型的人。
Lacoon isn't the only person betting on world models.

282
00:24:40,878 --> 00:24:44,176
Google DeepMind 有它的 Genie 世界模型，我最近也介绍过这个。
Google DeepMind has its Genie World models, which I covered recently.

283
00:24:44,622 --> 00:24:47,492
李飞飞的公司 World Labs 也在构建这些模型。
Feifei Li's company, World Labs, is building them too.

284
00:24:47,886 --> 00:24:52,416
9月，World Labs 展示了 Atlas，他们称之为全能世界模型。
In September, World Labs showed off Atlas, which it calls an Omni World model.

285
00:24:52,846 --> 00:25:00,260
你只需要给它几张照片，它就能构建出一个完整的3D
空间，你可以移动相机，甚至把模拟机器人放进去。
You can give it just a handful of photos, and it builds a whole 3D
space you can move a camera through and even drop simulated robots into.

286
00:25:00,686 --> 00:25:06,436
Nvidia 有用于机器人的世界模型，Meta 本身也在继续进行 JEPA 研究。
Nvidia has world models for robots, and Meta itself has continued working on JEPA research.

287
00:25:06,798 --> 00:25:11,936
所以这正在变成一场真正的竞赛，AI 的世界模型领域进展非常快。
So this is turning into a real race, and the world model side of AI is moving very fast.

288
00:25:12,206 --> 00:25:17,636
这就引出了第9点，也就是杨立昆的进展比人们意识到的要快得多。
Which leads me to point number 9, which is that Jan Lacun is moving a lot faster than people realize.

289
00:25:17,856 --> 00:25:25,156
很多人仍然认为杨立昆只是个在 X 上批评语言模型的人，但看看过去一年里实际发生了什么。
A lot of people still think of Lacun as the guy who just criticizes language models on X,
but look at what's actually happened over the last year.

290
00:25:25,582 --> 00:25:34,980
在2025年11月，杨立昆和 Balestriero 发布了 La Geppa，这是一种更简单、
更可靠的训练模型的方法，同时杨立昆宣布计划离开 Meta。
In November of 2025, Lacun and Balestriero released La Geppa, a simpler and more
reliable way to train these models, and Lacun announced plans to leave Meta.

291
00:25:35,438 --> 00:25:38,468
12月，AMI Labs 在巴黎正式成立。
In December, AMI Labs was officially founded in Paris.

292
00:25:38,958 --> 00:25:42,500
3月，它筹集了超过10亿美元并发布了 Le World Model。
In March, it raised over a billion dollars and released Le World Model.

293
00:25:42,926 --> 00:25:47,332
5月，团队发布了证明 La Geppa 实际上如何学习世界模型的的研究。
In May, the team posted research proving when La Geppa actually learns a world model.

294
00:25:47,694 --> 00:25:52,100
10月出现了 HJEPA，接着在10月底发布了这篇论文。
In October came HJEPA, and then at the end of October, this paper.

295
00:25:52,590 --> 00:26:01,136
这就是一个分布在巴黎、纽约、蒙特利尔和新加坡，只有大约50到60名研究人员的实验室，
每隔几个月就有一个重大的新成果。
That's a new major result every couple of months from a lab with only around
50 or 60 researchers spread across Paris, New York, Montreal, and Singapore.

296
00:26:01,710 --> 00:26:03,300
而这是一个更重要的教训。
And this is the bigger lesson.

297
00:26:03,662 --> 00:26:06,084
在目前的 AI 领域，任何实验室都能快速推进。
In AI right now, any lab can move fast.

298
00:26:06,510 --> 00:26:09,796
我们看到 Google 在大约一个月内从被看低到成为第一。
We've seen Google go from being written off to number one in about a month.

299
00:26:10,222 --> 00:26:14,276
我们看到小型中国实验室发布的模型可以与美国最大的公司竞争。
We've seen small Chinese labs ship models that compete with the biggest American companies.

300
00:26:14,734 --> 00:26:19,044
一个拥有正确想法的小团队可以超越资金多出一百倍的公司。
A small team with the right idea can leapfrog companies with a hundred times more money.

301
00:26:19,374 --> 00:26:26,564
而杨立昆拥有10亿美元、明确的研究方向以及世界上最优秀的研究人员之一，
正是这种能够做到这一切的团队。
And Lacun, with a billion dollars, a clear research direction, and some of the best researchers in the world,
is exactly the kind of team that could do it.

302
00:26:27,022 --> 00:26:34,020
没有人真的会退出 AI 竞赛，而那些看起来在场外的人，有时反而可能是领先最远的人。
Nobody is ever really out of the AI race, and the people who look like
they're on the sidelines can sometimes be the ones who are furthest ahead.

303
00:26:34,574 --> 00:26:37,412
杨立昆也知道，与众不同正是核心所在。
Lacoon also knows that being different is the whole point.

304
00:26:37,870 --> 00:26:39,136
这是杨立昆对此的看法。
Here's what Lacun said about it.

305
00:26:39,534 --> 00:26:40,976
那么我们该如何弥补这个差距呢？
So how do we bridge that gap?

306
00:26:41,056 --> 00:26:44,896
我们如何让系统去学习物理世界模型？
How do we get systems to learn models of the physical world?

307
00:26:45,198 --> 00:26:54,660
这将需要非生成式的全新架构，所以我告诉那些不搞生成
模型的人，所以他们都觉得我疯了，但我真的相信这一点。
And that will require new architectures that are not generative, so I'm telling people that don't work on generative models,
so they all think I'm crazy, but I really believe this.

308
00:26:54,976 --> 00:26:57,056
正如你所说，我正试图抢占先机。
And as you say, I'm trying to be ahead of the game.

309
00:26:57,376 --> 00:26:58,468
他们都觉得我疯了。
They all think I'm crazy.

310
00:26:58,894 --> 00:27:02,116
但杨立昆真的相信这一点，并且正试图抢占先机。
But Lacoon really believes it and is trying to be ahead of the game.

311
00:27:02,606 --> 00:27:06,820
这就引出了第10点，这也是为什么当它被扩大规模时，可能依然会崩溃的原因。
Which leads me to point number 10, which is why this could still break when it's scaled up.

312
00:27:07,374 --> 00:27:11,236
我认为这非常重要，这也是区分真实兴奋与炒作的关键。
I think this is really important, and it's what separates real excitement from hype.

313
00:27:11,790 --> 00:27:16,580
AI领域的很多想法在小规模时表现出色，但一旦扩大规模就会崩溃。
A lot of ideas in AI work brilliantly when they're small and then fall apart when they're scaled up.

314
00:27:17,070 --> 00:27:28,420
AI领域有一篇著名的文章叫 Rich Sutton 的《苦涩的教训》，它反复论证，
最终获胜的方法是那些能最大化利用更多算力的方法，而不是巧妙的设计。
There's a famous essay in AI called The Bitter Lesson by Rich Sutton, which argues that over and over again,
the methods that win are the ones that make the most of more computing power rather than clever designs.

315
00:27:29,134 --> 00:27:31,812
大语言模型就是这一点的终极范例。
Large language models have been the ultimate example of that.

316
00:27:32,334 --> 00:27:38,148
所以杨立昆的方法仍需证明，它在更大的规模上依然有效且能保持优势。
So Lacoon's approach still has to prove that it keeps working and keeps its advantage at a much larger scale.

317
00:27:38,574 --> 00:27:39,812
还有其他挑战。
There are other challenges too.

318
00:27:40,174 --> 00:27:45,348
今年5月，一项独立研究测试了多种世界模型设计，发现它们异常脆弱。
In May, a separate study tested several world model designs and found that they were surprisingly brittle.

319
00:27:45,774 --> 00:27:51,108
微小的视觉变化，比如改变物体的颜色或背景，就会让它们的表现大幅下降。
Small visual changes, like changing the colour of an object or the background, made them perform a lot worse.

320
00:27:51,790 --> 00:27:57,700
AMI Labs 自己也表示它是一家研究公司，而且到目前为止的结果只是研究结果，不是产品。
AMI Labs itself has said it's a research company, and that its results so far are research results, not products.

321
00:27:58,350 --> 00:28:10,148
杨立昆表示，最早的商业用途可能在工业领域，比如控制发电厂、喷气发动机和化工厂，
在这些领域，世界模型可以充当数字孪生，在问题发生前就预测出来。
Lacoon has said the earliest commercial uses will probably be in industry, things like controlling power plants, jet engines,
and chemical plants, where a world model can act like a digital twin that predicts problems before they happen.

322
00:28:10,702 --> 00:28:13,348
消费类产品和家用机器人还比较遥远。
Consumer products and home robots are further away.

323
00:28:13,870 --> 00:28:15,044
所以这可能会失败。
So this could break.

324
00:28:15,470 --> 00:28:18,628
数学上可能成立，但实际收益可能会随着规模扩大而缩减。
The maths could hold, but the practical gains could shrink at scale.

325
00:28:19,118 --> 00:28:23,716
或者那些大语言模型实验室可能直接借用好的创意，并把它们整合到自己的模型中。
Or the big language model labs could simply borrow the good ideas and fold them into their own models.

326
00:28:24,014 --> 00:28:25,700
这种情况在 AI 领域经常发生。
That happens all the time in AI.

327
00:28:26,158 --> 00:28:29,828
但即便如此，我认为这项研究依然非常有前景。
But even with all of that, I think this research is genuinely promising.

328
00:28:30,190 --> 00:28:37,412
它不仅仅是另一个更大的模型，它是一个拥有数学解释且结果指向正确方向的不同思路。
It's not just another bigger model, it's a different idea with a mathematical
explanation behind it and results that point in the right direction.

329
00:28:37,998 --> 00:28:39,300
所以，我将关注以下几点。
So here's what I'll be watching.

330
00:28:39,790 --> 00:28:42,884
首先，其他研究人员能否复现这些结果。
First, whether other researchers can reproduce these results.

331
00:28:43,502 --> 00:28:47,844
其次，当 AMI Labs 训练更大的 JEPA 模型时会发生什么？
Second, what happens when AMI Labs trains much bigger JEPA models?

332
00:28:48,398 --> 00:28:52,420
第三，这种方法是否开始出现在真实的机器人和真实产品中。
Third, whether this approach starts showing up in real robots and real products.

333
00:28:52,814 --> 00:28:56,484
第四，那些大型实验室是否会开始悄悄采用这些想法。
And fourth, whether the big labs start quietly adopting these ideas.

334
00:28:56,878 --> 00:29:00,132
因为如果他们真的这么做了，那将是 LeCun 坚持正确的最明确信号。
Because if they do, that'll be the clearest sign that Lacun was right.

335
00:29:00,750 --> 00:29:02,052
所以，这是我的真实看法。
So here's my honest take.

336
00:29:02,574 --> 00:29:06,372
我不认为杨立昆一夜之间就证明了整个 AI 行业是错的。
I don't think Jan Lacoon has proven the whole AI industry wrong overnight.

337
00:29:06,830 --> 00:29:10,532
语言模型极其好用，而且它们还会变得越来越强。
Language models are incredibly useful and they're going to keep getting better.

338
00:29:10,894 --> 00:29:15,460
但我确实认为 LeCun 揭示了 AI 行业可能最大的盲点。
But I do think Lacun has exposed what might be the AI industry's biggest blind spot.

339
00:29:15,792 --> 00:29:21,316
那就是：认为让 AI 理解世界的唯一方法就是把模型做大，并喂给它更多的文本。
The assumption that the only way to make AI understand the world is to make it bigger and feed it more text.

340
00:29:21,838 --> 00:29:26,500
这篇论文表明，AI 是如何学习的，可能和它有多大一样重要。
This paper suggests that how an AI learns might matter just as much as how big it is.

341
00:29:26,830 --> 00:29:32,032
如果这是真的，那么 AI 下一次巨大的飞跃可能不会来自拥有最大数据中心的公司。
And if that's true, the next big leap in AI might not come from the company with the biggest data center.

342
00:29:32,192 --> 00:29:34,352
它可能来自拥有最好想法的公司。
It might come from the company with the best idea.

343
00:29:34,606 --> 00:29:38,468
LeCun 多年来一直被指责为固执、反其道而行之，而且是错的。
Lacoon has spent years being called stubborn, contrarian, and wrong.

344
00:29:38,894 --> 00:29:43,108
但 LeCun 以前也对过，就在几乎没人相信神经网络的时候。
But Lacoon has been right before, back when almost nobody believed in neural networks.

345
00:29:43,534 --> 00:29:46,852
如果这项研究能够规模化，LeCun 最后可能又是正确的。
And if this research scales, Lakun might end up being right again.

346
00:29:47,406 --> 00:29:48,832
在评论区告诉我你的想法。
Let me know in the comments what you think.

347
00:29:49,230 --> 00:29:53,124
杨立昆关于世界模型的观点是对的，还是最终还是更大的语言模型胜出？
Is Yan Lakun right about world models, or will bigger language models win in the end?

348
